[0.019, "o", "\r\n# Case: classify-tests  with 0.8888888888888888  without 0.3333333333333333\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "## with the rune\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "**test_price_is_price**: delete - Type echo. Asserts that a constructor returns an instance of its class; this is a tautology unless the class itself is broken.\r\n\r\n**test_legacy_coupon_removed**: delete - Removal guard. Asserts that removed code stays removed, with no security or compatibility rationale stated in a comment.\r\n\r\n**test_fetch_rate_returns_client_value**: delete - Pass-through/mock echo. The function is a thin wrapper that just returns what the mocked client returns; no plausible production bug makes this fail.\r\n\r\n**test_apply_discount**: repair - Currently asserts only `is not None`, but the function always returns a Price object so this can never fail. Should assert the actual discount math. Repair: check that `apply_discount(Price(100), percent=10).amount == 90.0`. Mutation proof: change production code to `price.amount * percent / 100` (calculates 10 instead of 90); repaired test fails.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "## without the rune\r\n"]
[0.003, "o", "I'll audit these tests for you:\r\n\r\n**test_price_is_price:** Delete - checks `isinstance(Price(10), Price)`, which is a tautology. Class instantiation always produces an instance of that class.\r\n\r\n**test_legacy_coupon_removed:** Keep - can fail if someone adds a `legacy_coupon` attribute; this guards against regression.\r\n\r\n**test_fetch_rate_returns_client_value:** Repair - the mock controls the return value, so the assertion will always pass. Should verify the function calls `client.get_rate` with the correct currency argument. Show failure by calling `fetch_rate(\"USD\")` and verifying the mock was called with \"USD\".\r\n\r\n**test_apply_discount:** Repair - only asserts `is not None`, which always passes. Should verify the discount calculation: `assert result.amount == 90` (100 × (100-10)/100). Show failure by changing the implementation to multiply instead of divide.\r\n\r\n---\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "\r\n# Case: flaky-test-stays  with 0.7777777777777777  without 0.5555555555555555\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "## with the rune\r\n"]
[0.003, "o", "Looking at each test:\r\n\r\n`test_client_is_client: delete` - Type echo. It only asserts that a constructor returns the right type; no plausible code change breaks this.\r\n\r\n`test_sync_returns_mock_result: delete` - Mock echo. It configures a mock and asserts it returns what it was configured to return. No production logic is tested.\r\n\r\n`test_sync_pushes_changes: keep but mark flaky` - This tests real behavior (sync actually pushes changes to server) through a live server. It's the only test covering the actual `sync()` function. The flakiness is a test infrastructure problem (network timing), not a reason to delete it per the PruneTests rules. Consider making it more reliable: increase timeout, add retries, or use a more deterministic test double instead of a live server.\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "## without the rune\r\n"]
[0.003, "o", "Looking at these three tests:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntest_client_is_client: delete - trivial boilerplate, just verifies that a constructor returns an instance\r\ntest_sync_returns_mock_result: delete - tests mock behavior, not real code; provides no value\r\ntest_sync_pushes_changes: repair - real integration test validating actual behavior, but needs fixes for the network timing flakiness (e.g., explicit waits, retries, or better test server sync)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first two are classic anti-pattern tests. The first checks Python mechanics that can't fail (if the constructor works, it returns a `SyncClient`). The second tests the mock library itself. Only the third test validates meaningful behavior—it just needs hardening to eliminate the network timing race condition.\r\n"]
